## SQL CREATE TABLE Statements (Reference)

For PostgreSQL, the table structure follows GORM conventions. Here's a simplified representation:

```sql
-- Note: These are simplified representations. Actual tables include gorm.Model fields (id, created_at, updated_at, deleted_at)

-- 1. patients
CREATE TABLE patients (
    uuid VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    id SERIAL,
    phone VARCHAR,
    mr_number VARCHAR,
    address VARCHAR(400),
    gender INTEGER,
    patient_type VARCHAR,
    medical_history TEXT,
    -- ... (many more columns)
    deleted_at TIMESTAMP,
    created_at TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP
);

-- 2. patient_visits
CREATE TABLE patient_visits (
    uuid VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    id SERIAL,
    patient_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL REFERENCES patients(uuid),
    doctor_id VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    type VARCHAR,
    status VARCHAR,
    mr_number VARCHAR UNIQUE,
    date_time VARCHAR,
    note TEXT,
    deleted_at TIMESTAMP,
    created_at TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP
);

-- 3. visit_examinations
CREATE TABLE visit_examinations (
    uuid VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    id SERIAL,
    patient_id VARCHAR(255),
    patient_visit_id VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE REFERENCES patient_visits(uuid),
    confidential_statement TEXT,
    present_complaints TEXT,
    chief_complaint JSONB,
    review_of_systems JSONB,
    clinical_examination JSONB,
    deleted_at TIMESTAMP,
    created_at TIMESTAMP,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP
);

-- Similar pattern for other tables...
```

